---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('omnisend')!;
const b = appBySlug('drip')!;
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-drip';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/omnisend-vs-drip');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/omnisend-vs-drip');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Drip only recover shoppers who reach checkout?",
    "answer": "No. Drip documents Updated a Cart tracking for identified shoppers, alongside checkout-created and checkout-updated triggers. Enable add-to-cart tracking in the Shopify integration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will the reminder show the newest basket?",
    "answer": "Validate it with an actual triggered message. Drip documents that additions after an email is queued will not appear in that email; Omnisend does not track product removal from cart."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both recovery systems send during migration?",
    "answer": "Assign one sender to each stage and reconcile pending messages before cutover. Keep the replacement restricted to test contacts until purchase exits and recovery links pass."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the basket figures vendor results?",
    "answer": "No. They are fixed-goods-cost gross-profit examples, excluding fulfillment, payment fees, messaging, returns and platform costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare a combined cart-and-checkout email journey with separate stage workflows, then prove what happens when the basket changes.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Omnisend", "reason": "Keep separate cart and checkout stages with email, SMS and push available in the recovery workflow."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Use identified cart activity and checkout events in a configurable email recovery journey."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Choose the operating model before moving a working flow</h2>
<p>Omnisend suits a merchant who wants cart and checkout recovery to be distinct stages and wants to evaluate channel steps in the same workflow. Drip deserves a closer look when the email team wants cart and checkout activity feeding a configurable recovery journey. The useful comparison is how each handles a shopper changing their mind, basket and purchase stage while a message waits. A generic automation score cannot answer that question.</p>
<h2>Drip can receive cart activity before checkout</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">Drip’s cart-versus-checkout guide</a> distinguishes Updated a Cart, which requires an identified shopper, from events sent when the shopper reaches checkout. Enable add-to-cart tracking in the Shopify integration. Newly installed abandoned-cart workflows include the cart trigger; an older workflow may need it added. Drip’s product block reads recent cart activity, but products added after an email is queued do not appear in that queued email.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695514509-Abandoned-Cart-Workflow">Drip’s recovery template</a> includes cart and checkout triggers. It permits one active enrollment per person in that workflow, allows re-entry after exit and uses an Order Created goal to skip remaining steps. New triggers apply going forward. This matters when replacing an existing sender: importing contacts does not mean yesterday’s abandoned baskets automatically enter the new workflow.</p>
<h2>Omnisend separates the stages, with a basket-state limitation</h2>
<p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/6659889-abandoned-cart-and-abandoned-checkout-automations">Omnisend’s recovery guide</a> distinguishes cart from checkout abandonment for identified contacts. It supports email, SMS and push steps, but does not track product removal from cart. Its abandoned-product image button leads to a product page; use a separate recovery button for the whole basket. An editor test has no event payload, so use a restricted store-triggered message to check the destination. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Acceptance exercise: the changing basket</h2>
<p>These are proposed checks, not observed supplier tests. With an identified test shopper, add a large and a small variant, wait until an email is queued, remove the small variant and add a different size. Record the event times, queued message time, rendered items and recovery destination. In Drip, distinguish a legitimate queue snapshot from a missing cart event. In Omnisend, do not assume a removed product disappears from the reminder. Set customer expectations with a clear link to review the live basket rather than promising every pictured item is still selected or available.</p>
<p>Repeat with a shopper first identified at checkout. Move from cart to checkout before the first delay ends, then buy before the next send. Require one intended stage owner and no subsequent recovery message. Check duplicate events and a genuinely new basket after exit separately: blocking every repeat buyer would hide a re-entry defect. With Omnisend, inspect the earlier flow’s checkout exit; with Drip, inspect the goal and enrollment history in the installed workflow.</p>
<h2>Turn the original basket scenarios into useful branches</h2>
<p>For a B2B basket below minimum order quantity, explain the minimum and link to eligible pack sizes; do not try to solve a quantity rule with a percentage code. For a repeat buyer with a saved address, shorten the path back to review instead of repeating new-customer reassurance. For international shipping shock, show how to obtain the destination quote and duty information without promising free delivery everywhere.</p>
<p>For a subscription toggle left on, link to an editable basket and explain the available purchase options. For Instagram mobile traffic, check that the recovery link survives the in-app browser and that the next tap reaches the intended basket. For unavailable variants, offer an appropriate stock notification or available alternative rather than asserting a discount makes the original basket purchasable.</p>
<p>Gift-message and sizing friction calls for an edit path, size guide and support contact. An opened financing widget or abandoned BNPL step calls for payment-option help, without implying approval or inventing urgency. For a broken bundle price, repair eligibility and explain combinations; test welcome-code stacking before authorizing a second incentive. Keep purchase exits separate from refunds: a partial refund belongs in post-purchase support and net-sales reporting, not automatic re-enrollment into the old cart sequence.</p>
<h2>Preserve margin without pretending this is a performance test</h2>
<p>For the original hypothetical $550 basket at 61% gross margin, fixed goods costs leave $335.50 gross profit before an incentive. A 15% code costs $82.50 and leaves $253.00. For the $564 basket at 36% margin, a 14% code costs $78.96, reducing gross profit from $203.04 to $124.08. Neither figure is contribution after operating costs, and neither discount is a recommended cap. Fulfillment, returns, payment fees and message costs still need to be deducted.</p>
<h2>Keep email recovery useful before adding another channel</h2>
<p>The Drip evidence here establishes email recovery, not equivalent native SMS features. If your Drip design adds a separate SMS provider, price that provider and its event handoff explicitly. In Omnisend, verify channel subscription handling at each step so an email-only contact continues along the intended path. Set delays and quiet-hour handling for the actual audience; there is no evidence for the old page’s universal minute-by-minute ladder.</p>
<h2>Cut over with an event ledger and a rollback owner</h2>
<p>List Shopify reminders, the old provider, welcome messages and any separate SMS sender. For each stage, record who can enroll, who can send and who cancels on purchase. Reconcile pending sends before disabling the prior owner and enabling the replacement. Preserve permissions, exclusions and capture sources. If rendering or the order goal fails, stop new enrollment and account for queued sends before restoring the previous owner.</p>
<p>Judge the change by identified eligible shoppers, delivered reminders, unique Shopify orders, discounts, refunds and operating cost. Use a randomized holdout sized for your traffic if measuring incremental recovery; keep its assignment stable across stages. A platform attribution window can describe credited orders, but does not establish causality. Prefer the existing system if the proposed change adds maintenance without solving a demonstrated cart-state or workflow problem.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
